WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.055 --> 00:00:02.180
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Service
Level Objectives, or SLOs,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.555
are one of the fundamental
principles of site reliability

00:00:04.555 --> 00:00:05.470
engineering.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:06.870
We use them to
precisely quantify

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.700
the reliability target we want
to achieve in our service.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.050
But how do we measure them?

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.050
And what do we do in our
SLO monitoring tells us

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.400
that there's a problem?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.370
With Service Monitoring we can
track SLOs and error budgets

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.980
and quickly respond when our
service is having an issue.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.879
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:24.813
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.680
First, let's review the basics.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.319
You may have heard of
SLOs, but what's an SLI?

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:35.720
Let's clearly define
both of those.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.350
Our overall objective here is to
accurately quantify reliability

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.330
and measure our user happiness.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.520
The actual thing we'll
use as a proxy for that

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.330
is called the Service
Level Indicator, or SLI.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.650
We need to define that
indicator for our service.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.390
An easy place to
start is by using

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.808
one of our golden signals.

00:00:51.808 --> 00:00:54.100
We'll put the link to the
Monitoring chapter of the SRE

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.597
book where those are
explained in the notes below.

00:00:56.597 --> 00:00:59.180
You can also watch the excellent
video created by Liz and Seth

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.270
that explains this
topic in greater detail.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.870
We'll link to that, too.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.420
Generally, SLIs fall
into two categories.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.580
Request-based SLIs
measure the fraction

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.070
of good units of work
against the total.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.620
For example, what percentage of
a request to an HTTP endpoint

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.870
were successful on
a given time window?

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.050
Window-based SLIs measure the
fraction of time intervals

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.570
during which the service meets
some threshold over a given

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.980
time window.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.545
Let's use an example.

00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:24.920
If we have an app
engine service,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.680
we can use the response
count metric as our SLI.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.170
Specifically, we can
count our good responses

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.390
by filtering that metric
for only responses that

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:36.110
return an HTTP 200 and use
the unfiltered value to count

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.790
our total responses.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.030
We divide the two to arrive at
the fraction, or ratio of good

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:41.900
to total.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.940
And that's our SLI.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.450
Now let's talk about SLOs.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.170
A Service Level Objective
is the target value

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.540
for our SLI measured over time.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.050
It's generally comprised
of three things, the SLI,

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.240
a target for the SLI, and a
window over which it's measured

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.980
called the compliance period.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.470
So for our simple
HTTP service, we

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:05.120
may set our SLO as 99% of
requests return HTTP 200

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.009
for the last 28 days.

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:08.509
Our error budget
then is essentially

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.039
the inverse of the SLO.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.830
In this example, we're expecting
that 1% of our requests

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.240
will fail every day.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.980
Now that we have
that sorted out,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.397
let's take a look at how we
can use the Service Monitoring

00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:21.620
API to create, track,
and alert on our SLOs.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.860
First, we define our SLI
and SLO using a JSON payload

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.750
and send it to the
API to create them.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.370
There are four
key elements here.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.770
The good service filter
and total service filter

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.480
are how we specify
how we're counting

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.400
good and total requests.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.860
The goal is the actual
objective for the SLO,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.330
the target we want to meet.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.700
And the rolling period is
the time window over which

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.070
we want to evaluate our SLI.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.100
Once our SLO is created, we can
then create an alerting policy,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.583
using SLO burn rate
as the condition type.

00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:50.750
We want to be notified if
our service consumes error

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.670
budget at an elevated rate.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.100
In the alerting policy
we specify the threshold,

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.080
the percentage of
error budget consumed,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.180
and the look back duration--
the period over which

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.980
that consumption can happen.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.777
If our service
experience is an issue,

00:03:02.777 --> 00:03:04.610
our alerting policy
will create an incident,

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.470
letting us know that our error
budget burn rate is too high.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.620
We then know that our
service quality has degraded

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.900
and we should take immediate
action to resolve the issue.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.192
Now that we have Service
Monitoring set up and alerting

00:03:14.192 --> 00:03:15.620
for service issues
configured, we

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.162
can be much more
confident that we'll

00:03:17.162 --> 00:03:19.310
be able to deliver on our
service level objectives

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.783
and keep our users happy.

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:21.950
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.180
That's it for this episode.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.590
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:25.580
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

